  "factory-patterns": `
## Why
Creation knowledge (which class, which config) in one place
callers use interfaces.
## Simple factory
create(Channel) → switch in one place.
Registry: EnumMap<Channel, Supplier<Notifier>> + register()
new type = one registration.
## Factory method (GoF)
Algorithm calls abstract createX()
subclasses decide. Or inject Supplier<X>.
Example: Collection.iterator()
Game.createBoard().
## Abstract factory
Family that must match: CloudKit {blobs, queue, secrets} → GcpKit, AwsKit, LocalKit.
## Static factories
Money.of, List.of, Optional.empty, Duration.ofMinutes: names, caching, subtypes, validation.
## Smells
Pointless factories; duplicated switches; god factory; abstract factory without a family.
`,
  "builder-prototype": `
## Builder
Required params in builder constructor
defaults in builder fields
fluent setters return this.
build(): validate combinations once; construct immutable product; copy collections.
Examples: HttpRequest.newBuilder(), StringBuilder, protobuf builders, @Builder.
Skip for 2–3 distinct params: constructor or record.
## Prototype
Copy a configured template instead of rebuilding.
Immutable: withX(...) methods; sharing is safe.
Mutable: shallow copy shares sub-objects (bugs)
deep copy copies them (decide depth).
Prefer copy constructors / copyOf over clone().
## Smells
Setter soup; builder without validation; builders for tiny objects; shallow copies of mutable state; product sharing builder's collections.
`,
  "singleton-pool": `
## Singleton
One instance + global access. Keep the first, drop the second.
Naive lazy: race → two instances.
Eager static final; holder idiom (lazy, lock-free); enum (best per Effective Java).
DCL correct only with volatile (else half-built object visible).
## Problems
Hidden dependencies
tests share state, can't fake
"only one" changes (regions, tenants).
Fix: create once in main, inject everywhere.
OK for stateless process-wide utilities.
## Object pool
Expensive create, cheap reuse: DB connections (HikariCP), threads, buffers.
BlockingQueue of idle objects; borrow(timeout); giveBack validates/replaces.
Bound + timeout + validation + reset + try/finally return.
Don't pool cheap objects.
`,
  "adapter-facade": `
## Adapter
Your interface ← adapter → vendor/legacy class.
Translates names, units (minor units), shapes, errors.
Only class importing the SDK; fakes in tests; sandbox integration tests for the adapter.
Object adapter (composition) preferred.
## Facade
One method for a multi-subsystem sequence + compensation (release reservation on failure).
Subsystems remain available. Not a home for business rules.
## Wrapper comparison
Adapter: different interface, fit. Facade: simpler interface, ease.
Decorator: same interface, add behaviour. Proxy: same interface, control access.
## Anti-corruption layer
System-scale adapter
legacy/other team's model stays outside.
## Smells
Vendor types in signatures; passed-through vendor errors; god facade; facade blocking advanced use.
`,
  "decorator-proxy": `
## Decorator
Same interface; wrap; before/forward/after.
Stack: Metrics(Retrying(Logging(Stripe))). Order changes behaviour.
Java I/O streams are decorators.
Retries need idempotency keys.
## Proxy
Virtual: create on first use (LazyImage). Remote: RPC stub (timeouts, idempotency).
Protection: check permission then forward. Caching: answer from stored results.
## Dynamic proxies / AOP
java.lang.reflect.Proxy + InvocationHandler.
Spring @Transactional/@Cacheable/@Retryable = generated proxies.
Self-invocation bypasses the proxy.
## Decorator vs proxy
Identical shape; intent differs: add behaviour vs control access.
## Smells
Fat wrapper; wrong order (cache outside auth); retrying non-idempotent calls; unsynchronised lazy init; self-invocation.
`,
  "composite-bridge-flyweight": `
## Composite
One interface for leaf and group
group.size() = sum of children.size().
add/remove only on the composite. Parent pointers consistent; reject cycles; cache aggregates if hot.
Uses: file systems, UI trees, org charts, bundles, menus, expression trees.
## Bridge
Two dimensions → hold one in the other via an interface.
2 shapes × 3 renderers: 6 subclasses vs 5 classes.
JDBC, notification × channel, logger × appender.
## Flyweight
Share intrinsic immutable state
pass extrinsic state per use.
Factory/pool with computeIfAbsent.
1M chars, 80 distinct glyphs → 80 shared objects.
Java: string interning, Integer cache, Boolean constants
games share tree models.
## Smells
instanceof in tree code; leaves with add(); cycles; inheritance for two dimensions; mutable flyweights; flyweights without need.
`,
